# Global rules for coding agents (`AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` / `GEMINI.md`)

## 1. Output and ownership
- **Answer first**: lead with the conclusion, then short structured points and file links; keep measured facts apart from inferences. Reply in the user's language.
- **Finish the job**: within the granted scope, implement, verify with targeted tests and clean up on your own; stop to ask only for the decisions in §5.
${SUPERVISOR_RULE}

## 2. Workspace (`${WORKSPACE_ROOT}`)
- **Layout**: `Projects/<project>` (one clone per repo, parked on `main`; read its own `AGENTS.md`) · `{Orca,Codex,Claude}/Worktrees/` (one worktree per task) · `Data/` (shared caches) · `Archive/<YYYY-MM-DD-topic>/` (retired work).
- **Project zones (`agent-governor`)**: `docs/` · `reports/<date-topic>/` · `outputs/<topic>/` · `work/{_scratch,_tests,retained}/`. Allocate temp/test folders via `agent-governor scratch|test-dir --topic <topic>`; keep project and worktree roots clean.
${VOLUME_RULE}

## 3. Isolation and guardrails (`agent-guard`)
- **One writer per directory**: parallel code changes each get their own Git worktree on `${BRANCH_PREFIX}/<topic>` without editing main clones on `main`, and never clone a repo twice or copy a project folder; other chats in the same directory only read or work strictly in turn.
- **Enforced guardrails**: destructive deletes, dangerous Git commands, force-pushes, restricted branch pushes and root temp clutter are blocked by `agent-guard` with safe alternatives; follow the hint when blocked and never bypass it. Archive unmerged or dirty worktrees to `Archive/` before moving them to the Trash.${RESTRICTED_RULE}

## 4. Names and test environments
- **Names**: `Project｜Topic` (full-width bar, no spaces) for Orca workspaces, worktrees and chat tabs.
- **Tests and builds**: launch as many disposable iOS simulators and QEMU VMs as needed through `agent-test-device` (auto-reclaimed on exit); put custom `-derivedDataPath` and temp build output under `work/_tests/`, `work/_scratch/` or `Data/` (shared simulators, LaunchServices hygiene and manual reclamation live in the `orca-workspace` skill).

## 5. Shipping (`agent-ship`)
- **Routine changes**: once targeted tests pass, commit on the task branch and run `agent-ship` without waiting to be asked. It rebases on `main`, scans for secrets, runs `~/.orca/ship.json` checks, pushes, opens the PR, waits for CI, merges the reviewed SHA and deletes the branch.
- **Stop for a decision** (`agent-ship --no-merge`, PR left for review) when a change: ① deletes, rewrites or migrates user data or a database schema; ② submits to an app store or publishes a release; ③ touches signing, entitlements, accounts, billing or secrets; ④ breaks a public interface or is a large cross-project refactor; ⑤ meets an ambiguous conflict, or CI still fails after two fixes.
